# cc-install credentials and settings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to `.env` (or just run: ccauth) and fill in ONE of the three
# sections below. `.env` is gitignored and should be chmod 600 — it holds
# secrets. Anything you set here is passed into the container, so both
# `ccdocker` (CLI) and `ccvscode` (browser IDE) pick it up.
#
# Full explanation: docs/CREDENTIALS.md
# Apply changes after editing:  ccrestart
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === Option 1: Claude account (subscription) — recommended for most people ===
#
# Nothing to put in this file. Leave every variable below commented out, run
# `ccdocker`, and sign in through the browser when Claude Code prompts you.
# The login is saved in the claude-config Docker volume and persists.
#
# IMPORTANT: if ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK is set below, it
# overrides the interactive login. Comment them out to use your Claude account.


# === Option 2: Anthropic API key (personal, billed to your own account) ======
#
# Create a key at https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...


# === Option 3: Amazon Bedrock (UVA / Batten AWS account) ====================
#
# Ask Batten IT for credentials and the region if you don't have them.
#
# CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=AKIA...
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1
#
# Only if you were given a temporary/session credential:
# AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=...
#
# If Batten IT gave you a specific Bedrock model or inference profile ID:
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0
#
# Using AWS SSO / a named profile from your host ~/.aws instead of keys?
# Set the profile name here AND mount ~/.aws — see
# docker-compose.override.yml.example.
# AWS_PROFILE=uva


# === Optional: bake the Claude Code VS Code extension into the image =======
#
# The browser IDE can install extensions itself (Extensions panel, or
# `code-server --install-extension`), and they persist across updates — so you
# normally don't need this.
#
# Set to 1 and run `ccupdate` to include the Claude Code extension in the image
# instead. Costs about 670 MB: the extension bundles its own per-platform Claude
# binary, duplicating the CLI already in the image at the same version. Mainly
# useful for IT building a standard image for a fleet.
#
# Only affects NEW installs — Docker seeds a volume from the image once, so an
# existing install keeps whatever extensions it already has.
#
# CC_INSTALL_VSCODE_EXTENSION=0


# === Optional: use a different folder for your files =======================
#
# By default your files live in the `workspace` folder inside this install.
# Point that anywhere you like instead — an existing projects folder, an external
# drive, wherever.
#
# Don't edit this by hand; run `ccpath` and it does the rest:
#   ccpath ~/Dev/projects     switch to that folder
#   ccpath --show             show the current one
#   ccpath --reset            go back to ./workspace
#
# `ccpath` uses an absolute path on purpose. Docker Compose does not expand `~`,
# and a relative path resolves against whatever directory you happened to be in —
# either would mount the wrong folder, or a folder literally named `~`.
#
# Inside the container your files are always at /home/claudeuser/workspace, so
# nothing in the browser IDE moves around when you change this.
#
# CC_WORKSPACE=/Users/you/Dev/projects


# === Optional: password-protect the browser IDE ============================
#
# code-server is published on 127.0.0.1 only, so by default it is reachable
# just from this machine and needs no password. Set one if you have exposed the
# port to your network (see docker-compose.override.yml.example) or if you want
# a second layer on a shared computer. `ccvscode` will then ask for it.
#
# CC_VSCODE_PASSWORD=
